WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
How can a population change when no individual changes its inherited trait?

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.740
Watch this schematic herd.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.020
Each animal keeps its color through life, but the colored shares can change when a

4
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.940
new generation replaces the old.

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:22.833
Individuals in one population are not identical.

6
00:00:24.193 --> 00:00:26.673
Some differences come from food or experience.

7
00:00:28.093 --> 00:00:29.093
Others are inherited.

8
00:00:30.493 --> 00:00:35.693
Only inherited differences can pass from parents to offspring, so those differences

9
00:00:35.693 --> 00:00:37.493
can shape later generations.

10
00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:44.077
Zoom into the population's gene pool: all its alleles, meaning versions of genes.

11
00:00:45.357 --> 00:00:47.197
A mutation can make a new allele.

12
00:00:48.657 --> 00:00:51.517
Gene flow can bring an allele from another population.

13
00:00:52.897 --> 00:00:54.697
Both can change what the pool contains.

14
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.550
Sexual reproduction does something different.

15
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.810
Meiosis and fertilization reshuffle alleles that already exist into new

16
00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:10.410
combinations. The offspring can differ from both parents without a new allele

17
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.410
appearing.

18
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:16.533
Now suppose teal and gold individuals leave offspring equally often.

19
00:01:18.073 --> 00:01:19.173
Predict the next generation.

20
00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:24.313
Will one color steadily take over, or will their shares simply wobble?

21
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:35.050
With equal reproductive success, neither color has a consistent advantage.

22
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.470
Chance can still make the shares wobble.

23
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.730
Yet the model has no directional push toward teal or gold.

24
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:49.073
Real populations usually produce more young than their environments can support.

25
00:01:50.353 --> 00:01:55.113
Food, space, predators, disease, and other limits mean many never reproduce.

26
00:01:56.493 --> 00:02:00.833
The surviving adults are only part of the story; reproduction matters too.

27
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:03.367
Now change the conditions.

28
00:02:05.107 --> 00:02:10.387
Suppose teal individuals leave more offspring because their inherited variation fits

29
00:02:10.387 --> 00:02:11.627
those conditions better.

30
00:02:12.887 --> 00:02:16.647
More teal markers pass through the survival-and-reproduction filter.

31
00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:20.627
The next generation starts with a larger teal share.

32
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.340
When inherited variations affect reproductive success, populations change across

33
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.580
generations. That process is natural selection.

34
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.000
It needs inherited variation and unequal reproductive success under the current

35
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.000
conditions.

36
00:02:39.683 --> 00:02:41.443
Follow one gold individual.

37
00:02:42.703 --> 00:02:43.983
It never turns teal.

38
00:02:45.343 --> 00:02:46.683
Follow one teal individual.

39
00:02:48.003 --> 00:02:49.223
It never turns gold.

40
00:02:50.343 --> 00:02:55.183
The changing pattern appears only when we compare the population across generations.

41
00:02:56.417 --> 00:02:59.957
Selection can work only with variation that is present.

42
00:03:01.237 --> 00:03:06.677
If every marker begins gold and no new teal allele appears or arrives, a teal

43
00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:08.797
advantage cannot change the population.

44
00:03:10.637 --> 00:03:12.977
Conditions cannot select a variant that is absent.

45
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:19.560
Whether a variation helps, harms, or has no effect often depends on the environment.

46
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.160
Selection does not plan ahead, and organisms do not change inherited traits because

47
00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.160
they need them.

48
00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:29.523
Return to the whole field.

49
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:35.623
Different individuals entered, some left more offspring, and the next generation has

50
00:03:35.623 --> 00:03:41.923
a different mix. The population changed because reproductive success differed, not

51
00:03:41.923 --> 00:03:43.543
because individuals transformed.
